  "result": "I wrote the 10-minute lesson for O3 as `lessons/0001-context-and-memory-in-an-agent.html`. I haven't checked its length against the 10-minute budget (about 1,500 words), and I haven't opened it in a browser.\n\n**What it covers**\n- **Context window:** the model sees only what the program resends on each call, and you are billed for all of it every time. A bigger window doesn't fix this, because accuracy drops as context grows (context rot).\n- **Between steps and sessions:** the message list carries state within one conversation. Nothing carries across sessions unless your program saves it and loads it back.\n- **Retrieval and notes:** a search tool pulls only the relevant manual passages in, and notes write facts out to storage, such as a student's training status.\n\n**How it's built**\n- Two figures, three practice checks with instant feedback, and a worked example on the CNC router question.\n- A function-argument analogy, since you relate to computer science.\n\n**Quiz and notes**\n- Quiz `0002` and its key `keys/0002.json` are ready: five items, with no answers shown. Ask when you want to take it.\n- I added the sources to `RESOURCES.md` and updated `NOTES.md`. Quiz 0001 is still ungraded.\n\nSources: the primary one is [Anthropic's context engineering post](https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents), with the [Claude context windows docs](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/context-windows) and the [LangChain short-term memory docs](https://docs.langchain.com/oss/python/langchain/short-term-memory) as support.\n\nAsk me anything about the lesson. Do you want the next lesson to cover how retrieval picks the right passage in a long manual?",
